# Executable acceptance specification (Reqnroll): a record says how it is
# stored, on the record itself. Written before the implementation.
@implemented
Feature: A record says how it is stored
  As an F# application developer
  I want every storage fact about a record to be on the record or follow from it
  So that nothing restates the record's name and fields in a second declaration

  Every table name is written, and every column name is derived. A stored
  record names its table with [<Table "...">] and its key is its field named
  Id. An integer key, or a typed id over one, is generated by the database;
  any other key is assigned by the application, and [<Assigned>] says an
  integer key is assigned too. Everything else follows from the types: a
  typed id refers to the record whose key it is; a list, a record or an
  optional record of a stored type that holds the owner's key is owned; a
  list of a stored type that does not is linked, through the join table its
  field names with [<JoinTable "...">]. What no type can say is an attribute
  on the field: [<Unique>], [<Version>], [<Generated>], [<NotReference>] and
  [<OrderedBy>]. The model lists the records that are not owned, and the
  records they own come with them. Anything the types leave ambiguous is an
  error when the model is built, never a guess.

  Background:
    Given a configured relational database
    And boards, cards, comments and labels stored by what their types say

  Rule: The types say how the records are stored

    Scenario: The tables follow from the records
      When I print the DDL of the stored model
      Then the DDL creates the tables boards, covers, cards, comments, labels and card_labels
      And the key of cards is generated by the database
      And the key of labels is assigned by the application
      And comments has a foreign key from card to cards

    Scenario: A list of records that hold the owner's key is owned
      When I insert a stored Card with the comments "first" and "second"
      And I read the Card back
      Then the Card has the comments "first" and "second"

    Scenario: An owned list is read in the order its field names
      When I insert a stored Card with comments written at 10:00, 09:00 and 11:00
      And I read the Card back
      Then its comments are read in the order 09:00, 10:00, 11:00

    Scenario: A list of stored records that do not hold the owner's key is linked
      Given the labels "bug" and "ui"
      When I insert a stored Card linked to both labels
      And I read the Card back
      Then the Card has the labels "bug" and "ui"
      And the labels table still has two rows

    Scenario: An optional record that holds the owner's key is owned
      When I insert a Board with a cover
      And I read the Board back
      Then the Board has its cover

    Scenario: A unique field is the field's error when duplicated
      Given a Board with the slug "roadmap"
      When I insert another Board with the slug "roadmap"
      Then the insert fails with a uniqueness error on Slug, naming boards_slug_key

    Scenario: A version field checks the record a change was made from
      Given a stored Card read from the database
      And another writer changes that Card's title and version
      When I change the title of the stored Card I read
      Then the change fails as a conflict

    Scenario: A generated field is filled by the database
      When I insert a stored Card
      Then the Card comes back with the database's creation time

    Scenario: A field marked as no reference refers to nothing
      When I list the references the stored model infers
      Then Comment.Card references Card
      And Card.Board references Board
      And Card.MovedFrom refers to nothing

    Scenario: A query reads a stored record by its type
      Given Cards with the titles "Write the spec" and "Review"
      When I read the Cards whose title starts with "Write"
      Then the Cards read are "Write the spec"

  Rule: What the types leave ambiguous is refused

    Scenario Outline: A model the types cannot describe is refused
      When I build a stored model of <records>
      Then the model is refused for <model>, saying "<message>"

      Examples:
        | records                                          | model      | message                                         |
        | a record without a table                         | Untabled   | names no table                                  |
        | a record without an Id                           | Keyless    | has no field named Id                           |
        | a list item holding the owner's key twice        | Ambiguous  | two fields of type HolderId                     |
        | a linked list without a join table               | Unjoined   | names no join table                             |
        | a linked record that is not in the model         | Linker     | list Linkee in Model.build                      |
        | an owned record that is also listed              | OwnedChild | is owned by OwnedParent                         |
        | a record owned by two owners                     | SharedPart | is owned by both                                |
        | an owned list ordered by a field it does not have | Misordered | has no field named Missing                     |
        | a list of records that are not stored            | Plain      | is a list of Unstored, which names no table      |
        | a record with two version fields                 | Doubled    | two fields marked Version                       |
